  "Name": "Baumeister1967-Fig13-single-tunnel",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Optical Tunneling and Its Applications to Optical Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 6, 897-904 (1967). doi:10.1364/AO.6.000897 - Table III, columns 'Figs. 13-15' (p. 902); Figs. 11-14 captions and text pp. 902-903",
  "Notes": "Single tunnel layer bandpass filter (induced-transmission analogue / MacNeille-like polarizer): one 1/3-wave n=1.35 tunnel layer between 1.65L H(LH)^4 matching stacks, transmitting p and rejecting s at 60 deg in glass of index 1.70\nSpecification: Transmit the p polarization in a band around lambda0 (admittance-matched to the isotransmittance contours of Fig. 11), reject s.\nReported: Fig. 13 (frequency lambda0/lambda 0.8-1.2): p transmittance ~1.0 (flat top) from about 0.95 to 1.0, ~0.2 near 0.85-0.9, ~0.07 near 1.1 rising to ~0.18 at 1.2, ~0.45 at 0.8; s transmittance essentially zero except narrow spikes (near 0.82 and ~1.03). Fig. 14: external angle -1.70 deg (curve a) and +1.70 deg (curve b), i.e. +-1.0 deg inside the prism, shift the p passband to ~0.95 and ~1.0 (plot). Text p. 903: peak T drops to 84 % if the tunnel layer is slightly absorbing (see Baumeister1967-Fig15-single-tunnel-absorbing).\nNotes: Thicknesses in quarter waves at lambda0 (Table III footnote) except layer 11, which has a physical thickness of one-third wave (printed 0.333) -> 333.333 nm at reference_nm = lambda0 = 1000 nm (the stack is non-dispersive, lambda0 arbitrary; plot vs lambda0/lambda). TWO PRINTED VALUES ARE CORRECTED HERE: Table III prints layer 9 = 3.300 (index 2.30) and layer 18 = 2.900 (index 1.70). The Fig. 11 caption gives the matching stack as 1.65L H(LH)^4 with L = 2.0 QW (n 1.70) and H = 1.3016 QW (n 2.30), i.e. from the tunnel layer outward 3.30, 1.302, 2.0, 1.302, ... on each side, and the printed table is otherwise symmetric; so layer 9 should be 1.302 and layer 18 should be 2.000. Own TMM check: with the corrections the p curve matches Fig. 13 (T = 1.00 at 0.9685, 0.915 at 0.95, 0.962 at 1.0, 0.21 at 0.9, 0.07 at 1.1, 0.18 at 1.2) and k = 0.01 gives a peak of 0.866 (paper 84 %); with the values as printed the p peak is only 0.56 at 1.04, which does not resemble Fig. 13. H printed as 1.302 (caption 1.3016); 1.302 used. Angle 60 deg inside the 1.70 medium (Fig. 11 caption). Media index 1.70 per Table III.\nLayers from Table III, columns 'Figs. 13-15' (p. 902); Figs. 11-14 captions and text pp. 902-903; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours p peak T 100 %; above 90 % of it from lambda0/lambda 0.949 to 1.005; paper p ~1.0 from lambda0/lambda 0.95 to 1.0 (Fig. 13).",
